2026-11-22 10:47:25 - Agent_20261122104346 - INFO - [+] Agent signaled completion: Reviewed the single CodeQL result for the BER decoder group (pyasn1/codec/ber/decoder.py:1521, inside `SingleItemDecoder`). Investigated the surrounding decoding state machine, `readFromStream`/`peekIntoStream` in pyasn1/codec/streaming.py, and all 18 `readFromStream(substrate, length, ...)` call sites. Conclusions:

1) The exact location CodeQL pointed at (line 1521) is the `SingleItemDecoder` class header/`defaultRawDecoder`/`supportIndefLength` declarations — merely a line-context marker with no allocation or sink present. Classified as FALSE POSITIVE.

2) The genuinely interesting sink in this function is the BER length-decoding block (~lines 1651-1678): an attacker-controlled length field (up to 2^126 for a 15-octet length encoding, with a code comment explicitly noting "missing check on maximum size") is passed into `readFromStream(substrate, length, options)` and into `AbstractSimplePayloadDecoder.substrateCollector`, so a tiny malicious BER blob can drive a very large read/allocation. There is no documented maximum-length limit, so the DoS potential is real but depends on the surrounding stream implementation (blocking vs non-blocking, whether the stream actually yields `length` bytes) and on the caller's own guards. Submitted as MANUAL REVIEW (CWE-400/CWE-664) rather than a confirmed true positive, at line 1650. No additional independent vulnerabilities were confirmed during this review.
